course: myths
lesson: 14
title: The Moon, the planets and the stars
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: A film voice booms: "The DARK side of the Moon, where sunlight never falls..." Spooky music, a cold black world. It's a wonderful shiver, and completely wrong.
explain: The Moon always shows us the same face, so the other side was "dark" in the old sense: unknown. It gets just as much sunshine as our side, and we first saw it in 1959, when a spacecraft photographed it.
explain: The night sky is full of myths like that, because we see everything from very far away: shooting stars that look like falling stars, the famous North Star that must be the brightest, Mercury that must be the hottest.
explain: Each sensible guess can be checked: shooting stars are space pebbles burning in our air, Sirius outshines Polaris, and Venus is hotter than Mercury because its thick air traps the heat.
explain: And you can enjoy the stories while knowing the science: make a wish on a shooting star, read your horoscope for fun, and still know which is astronomy.
fact: The far side of the Moon gets as much sunlight as the near side; "dark" meant unknown, and the far side was first photographed by the Soviet Luna 3 in 1959.
fact: Shooting stars are tiny bits of space rock and dust burning up in our air; real stars are enormous and very far away.
fact: Polaris, the North Star, is not the brightest star; Sirius is the brightest star in the night sky, and Polaris is useful because it stays almost still above the North Pole.
fact: Venus is the hottest planet, around 465 °C, because its thick air traps heat; Mercury has almost no air, so its night side gets very cold.
fact: In 2006 astronomers reclassified Pluto as a dwarf planet after finding other similar objects, and some scientists still argue about it.
fact: The Moon makes no light of its own; it reflects sunlight, and its phases come from how much of its lit half we can see.
fact: Stars twinkle because their light is bent by moving air in our atmosphere; seen from space, they don't twinkle.
fact: The Moon looks bigger near the horizon, but a photo or a coin at arm's length shows it's the same size; scientists still debate exactly why our brains see it so.
fact: Saturn is not the only planet with rings: Jupiter, Uranus and Neptune have faint rings too.
fact: Astronomy is the science of space; astrology is a belief about stars and personality, and tests haven't shown it predicts anything.
fact: A black hole's pull, from far away, is the same as any object of the same mass, so only things that come very close fall in; if the Sun became a black hole of the same mass, the Earth's orbit would not change.
exercise: A film says the dark side of the Moon never gets sunlight. What's true? | The far side gets as much sunlight as the near side | It is in darkness forever | It is lit only during eclipses | It is lit by the Earth's light only
exercise: Why was it called the "dark" side? | "Dark" meant unknown, as we never saw it from Earth | It is made of black rock | The Sun never shines on that half, so it stays black | It is always night there for every visitor
exercise: When did people first see photos of the far side of the Moon? | In 1959, from a Soviet spacecraft | In ancient Greece, with a telescope | In 1969, from Apollo 11 only | Never, it can't be photographed
exercise: Lucy says shooting stars are stars falling out of the sky. What are they? | Tiny bits of space rock burning up in our air | Real stars dropping to Earth | Fireworks from other planets | Sparks from satellites
exercise: Ibrahim says the North Star is the brightest star. Which star is brightest at night? | Sirius | Polaris | The Moon | Mercury
exercise: Why is the North Star so useful? | It stays almost still above the North Pole | It is the brightest star there is in the whole sky | It changes colour through the night to show the time | It is the nearest star to Earth after the Sun itself
exercise: Mason says Mercury is hottest because it's closest to the Sun. Which planet is hottest? | Venus | Mercury | Mars | Jupiter
exercise: Why is Venus hotter than Mercury? | Its thick air traps heat | It is closer to the Sun | It has volcanoes on every side | It spins much faster
exercise: Ruby says Pluto is a planet and always will be. What happened in 2006? | Astronomers reclassified it as a dwarf planet | Pluto was destroyed by a comet | Pluto was found to be just a lost moon of Neptune | Pluto was renamed Planet X
exercise: Arlo says the Moon makes its own light. What's true? | It reflects sunlight | It burns like a small star | It glows from hot lava inside | It stores sunlight and lets it out at night
exercise: What causes the Moon's phases? | How much of its sunlit half we can see | The Earth's shadow covering it every night | Clouds hiding parts of it | The Moon changing shape
exercise: Ella's poem says stars flicker like candles. Why do stars twinkle? | Moving air in our atmosphere bends their light | Stars really flicker on and off | Stars spin like lighthouses | Clouds pass in front of them many times every second
exercise: Dad says the low Moon only looks bigger. Is he right? | Yes, it's the same size; a coin at arm's length shows it | No, it's much closer to us when it sits near the horizon | No, it swells up when it rises | Yes, but only in winter
exercise: Zac says Saturn is the only planet with rings. What's true? | Jupiter, Uranus and Neptune have faint rings too | Only Saturn has rings | Every planet, including Earth, has thin hidden rings | Mars has the biggest rings
exercise: Phoebe says astrology and astronomy are the same. What's the difference? | Astronomy is the science of space; astrology is a belief | They're two names for the same science of stars and planets | Astrology is the newer science | Astronomy is about horoscopes
exercise: Kian says black holes suck in everything like a vacuum cleaner. What's true? | Only things that come very close fall in | They pull in the whole universe, bit by bit | They suck in planets from any distance at all | They only suck in light and leave everything else
test: If the Sun turned into a black hole of the same mass tomorrow, what would happen to the Earth's orbit? | It wouldn't change, though it would get dark and cold | The Earth would be sucked in at once | The Earth would fly straight off into deep space, out of orbit | The orbit would become twice as fast
test: "Mercury's closest to the Sun so it's DEFINITELY hottest, no question." What's a kind answer? | "I'd guess that too! But Venus wins because of its thick air." | "You're thick, it's obviously Mars." | "Yeah, no question at all." | "Planets don't have temperatures."
test: Ruby's mum learned nine planets at school and is cross about Pluto. What's fair to say? | Scientists changed the definition in 2006, and some still argue | Pluto was never real | Her school taught her lies on purpose | Pluto will be made a planet again next year, scientists promised
test: In a dark room, you shine a lamp at a ball and walk around it. What do you see? | The ball is lit only on the lamp's side, like Moon phases | The ball glows all over by itself | The ball goes dark when you look | The ball changes size
test: An astronaut on the Space Station looks at stars. What would she notice? | They don't twinkle, because there's no air to bend the light | They twinkle even more | They blink on and off | They look green
test: Lucy's sister wants to wish on a falling star. What's the nicest true thing to say? | It's a space pebble burning up, but make a wish anyway! | Don't wish, it's a real star crashing down to Earth right now | Wishes only work on planets | Hide, it might land on us
test: "Saturn's the only one with rings, everyone knows." Which word is the clue to check? | "Only" | "Saturn" | "Rings" | "Everyone"
test: Phoebe reads her horoscope every morning. What's a wise view? | It's fine to enjoy it, but it isn't science | Horoscopes are astronomy, so they're always true | She should be banned from reading it every morning | Astronomers write horoscopes, so they must be right
test: At the horizon the Moon looks huge; high up it looks small. How could you test it tonight? | Compare it with a coin held at arm's length, low and high | Measure it with a ruler held on the window glass each night | Ask the Moon to stay still | Take a photo of only the low Moon
test: A cartoon shows a black hole sucking in every star in the galaxy, like a drain. What's wrong? | From far away it pulls like any object of the same mass | Nothing, that's exactly what they do to every star near or far | Black holes push things away | Black holes don't have any gravity
test: Your scout leader says Polaris is the brightest star. What's the wise correction? | "It's the most useful, but Sirius is brighter!" | "Polaris doesn't exist." | "The Moon is the brightest star." | "You're a rubbish scout leader."
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the illumination of the far side of the Moon? | It receives sunlight to the same extent as the near side | It remains in perpetual darkness | It is illuminated solely by light reflected from the Earth | It is illuminated solely during lunar eclipses
exam: Identify the planet of the solar system possessing the highest surface temperature, together with the reason. | Venus, owing to its dense atmosphere which retains heat | Mercury, owing to its proximity to the Sun, the source of heat | Mars, owing to its red colouration | Jupiter, owing to its great size
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the phenomenon commonly termed a shooting star. | Small fragments of rock and dust consumed by heat in the atmosphere | Stars detached from their fixed positions in the sky and falling to Earth | Light reflected from artificial satellites | Distant stars undergoing explosion
exam: Ascertain the source of the light by which the Moon is observed. | Sunlight reflected from its surface | Light generated within its own interior | Light emitted by its volcanic activity | Light received from distant stars alone
